/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Picture the mode field as a wheel spoke turning round the centre of the bend.
   - Measured loss per turn follows an exponential law well, written αr = c₁e−c₂R, where R is the bend radius and c₁ (dB per turn) and c₂ (per mm) are fitted from measurements on one fiber at one wavelength.
   - A rough scale for the radius where loss becomes large is the critical radius.
   - Rc ≈ 3n₁²λ / [4π(n₁² − n₂²)3/2] Rc is the critical radius, in the unit of λ; n₁ and n₂ are the core and cladding indices; λ is the free-space wavelength.
   - For a single-mode fiber the field reaches much further into the cladding, and the estimate becomes
   - Rcs ≈ [20λ/(n₁ − n₂)3/2] (2.748 − 0.996λ/λc)−3 Rcs is the single-mode critical radius; λc is the cutoff wavelength of the fiber; λ, Rcs and λc share one length unit.
   - Both formulas say the same thing: a large index difference holds the field tightly and allows sharp bends, and a longer wavelength, which spreads the field, needs gentler ones.
   - Microbending comes from many small random bends of the axis, with periods of a millimetre or less and amplitudes of micrometres.
   - Bending loss rises with wavelength.
   - Macrobend loss is quoted per turn at one radius and one wavelength, so multiply by the number of turns before adding it to the budget.
*/
