/* book Worked example: Q: A 1550 nm fiber has a measured bend loss αr = 8e−0.6R dB per turn, R in mm, valid from 4 to 15 mm. (a) Find the loss of one turn at R = 5 mm and at R = 10 mm. (b) A tray forces four turns at R = 5 mm; find the added loss. (c) The same fiber passes a mandrel test on a 30 mm mandrel; what is the loss per turn?
   1. (a) At R = 5 mm: 0.6 × 5 = 3, e−3 = 0.04979, αr = 8 × 0.04979 = 0.398 dB per turn.
   2. At R = 10 mm: 0.6 × 10 = 6, e−6 = 0.002479, αr = 8 × 0.002479 = 0.0198 dB per turn. Doubling R cut the loss twentyfold.
   3. (b) Four turns at 5 mm: 4 × 0.398 = 1.59 dB, as much as 8 km of fiber at 0.2 dB/km.
   4. (c) A 30 mm mandrel means R = 15 mm: e−9 = 1.234 × 10−4, αr = 8 × 1.234 × 10−4 = 0.00099 dB per turn, too small to matter.
   Answer: 0.40 dB per turn at 5 mm and 0.020 dB at 10 mm; four turns at 5 mm add 1.6 dB; on the 30 mm mandrel the loss is about 0.001 dB per turn. */
