/* book Worked example 2: Q: Find the critical bend radius (a) of a multimode fiber with n₁ = 1.48 and n₂ = 1.46 at 850 nm, and (b) of a single-mode fiber with n₁ = 1.465, n₂ = 1.460 and cutoff λc = 1.26 µm, at 1550 nm and at 1310 nm.
   1. (a) n₁² − n₂² = 2.1904 − 2.1316 = 0.0588, and 0.05883/2 = 0.01426.
   2. Rc = 3 × 2.1904 × 0.85 µm ÷ (4π × 0.01426) = 5.586 ÷ 0.1792 = 31.2 µm.
   3. (b) n₁ − n₂ = 0.005, and 0.0053/2 = 3.536 × 10−4. At 1550 nm: 20λ/(n₁ − n₂)3/2 = 20 × 1.55 µm ÷ 3.536 × 10−4 = 87 680 µm = 87.7 mm.
   4. Bracket: 2.748 − 0.996 × 1.55/1.26 = 2.748 − 1.225 = 1.523, and 1.523−3 = 0.283. So Rcs = 87.7 × 0.283 = 24.8 mm.
   5. At 1310 nm: 20 × 1.31 ÷ 3.536 × 10−4 = 74.1 mm; bracket 2.748 − 1.036 = 1.712, cubed inverse 0.199; Rcs = 74.1 × 0.199 = 14.8 mm.
   Answer: The multimode fiber can bend to about 31 µm before loss is large; the single-mode fiber needs about 25 mm at 1550 nm and 15 mm at 1310 nm, which is why single-mode bend rules are tested at the longer wavelength. */
